{
  "dom_id": "step-e86",
  "name": "Example: Identifying When a Rational Function has no Vertical Asymptotes",
  "problem": "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)=\\dfrac{x^2+x}{2x+2}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x}{2x+2} & =\\frac{x(x+1)}{2(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cancel{(x+1)}}{2\\cancel{(x+1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/eda5457edafe14fb51fcc75ca75677b199bfc3e0aef8940c66f8aa78cf8cd5c3.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
